ayId":"401858303331","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7168,"playId":"401858303334","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7112,"playId":"401858303339","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.743,"playId":"401858303337","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7489,"playId":"401858303342","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7228,"playId":"401858303347","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7213,"playId":"401858303351","tiePercentage":0},{"homeWinPercentage":0.7303,"playId":"401858303356","tiePercentage":0}],"gameInfo":{"venue":{"id":"3982","guid":"4a5fe271-7ce8-3f94-8241-4a282763e8fc","fullName":"Wallace Wade Stadium","address":{"city":"Durham","state":"NC","zipCode":"27708","country":"USA"},"grass":true,"images":[{"href":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/venues/college-football/day/3982.jpg","width":2000,"height":1125,"alt":"","rel":["full","day"]},{"href":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/venues/college-football/day/interior/3982.jpg","width":2000,"height":1125,"alt":"","rel":["full","day","interior"]}]},"attendance":25311},"article":{"headline":"Clemson's QB rotation of Pearman and Denson, defense help the Tigers beat Duke 20-10","description":"— Trent Pearman and Chris Denson each ran for touchdowns while rotating at quarterback for injury-hit Clemson, helping the Tigers beat Duke 20-10 on Friday night to close their Atlantic Coast Conference schedule.","story":"DURHAM, N.C. -- — <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5081434/trent-pearman\" target=\"_new\">Trent Pearman</a> and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5210003/chris-denson\" target=\"_new\">Chris Denson</a> each ran for touchdowns while rotating at quarterback for injury-hit Clemson, helping the Tigers beat Duke 20-10 on Friday night to close their Atlantic Coast Conference schedule.\n\n\r\n              The duo provided enough punch for the Tigers (7-4, 5-3 ACC) on a grinding offensive night for both teams, while Clemson's defense kept Heisman Trophy candidate <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5164332/nate-sheppard\" target=\"_new\">Nate Sheppard</a> largely in check.\n\n\r\n              “Somebody else was going to have to beat us,\" Clemson coach Dabo Swinney said of Sheppard. \"That was the mindset that they had.”\n\n\r\n              Clemson held Duke to 247 total yards. That included a key stop with <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/4832060/jahiem-lawson\" target=\"_new\">Jahiem Lawson</a> coming on a blitz unchecked through the line to bat down <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/4694149/walker-eget\" target=\"_new\">Walker Eget</a>'s fourth-down pass with 3:34 left in a one-possession game.\n\n\r\n              Pearman, who arrived as a walk-on in 2022, threw for 164 yards and ran for 51 more in his first career start. That included getting around the left side and beating a defender to the pylon for a 9-yard score and 10-0 third-quarter lead.\n\n\r\n              Denson, a former quarterback who had converted to college receiver, made his own mark while flipping back to QB for this one. He ran 16 times for 71 yards, including when he bounced a keeper around the left side and hugged the sideline to score from the 7 for a 17-7 lead midway through the fourth.\n\n\r\n              Sheppard ran for 116 yards to lead the Blue Devils (7-4, 4-4). Much of that came when he popped a 55-yard TD run through the right side, avoiding a last-ditch tackle attempt from Clemson's diving <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/4917980/ronan-hanafin\" target=\"_new\">Ronan Hanafin</a> at the sideline to answer Pearman's score to get Duke within 10-7.\n\n\r\n              Otherwise, Sheppard ran for 61 yards on 18 carries, an average of 3.4 yards per carry.\n\n\r\n              “We had the long explosive touchdown run by Nate,” Duke coach Manny Diaz said. “We did hit some explsoive passes. But our ability to be efficient and stay on the field; we were unable to do that.”\n\n\r\n              <hl2>The takeaway</hl2>\r\n              Clemson: The Tigers were coming off a home loss to a two-win Georgia Tech team, which included seeing quarterbacks <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5159078/tait-reynolds\" target=\"_new\">Tait Reynolds</a> and <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/4899369/christopher-vizzina\" target=\"_new\">Christopher Vizzina</a> lost to injuries. That added to the building frustration for the Tigers and Swinney, though Swinney said that the criticism from the past week had served to “galvanize” our team on a night that ended with him hugging multiple staffers as the final seconds elapsed.\n\n\r\n              Duke: Sheppard entered Friday leading the country in all-purpose yardage (171.9) and ranking second in rushing (132.0). The program has also launched a campaign promoting him for the Heisman, which included a drone show after Friday's first quarter. But he got little help offensively, with Eget completing just 16 of 38 passes for 149 yards.\n\n\r\n              “Especially in the first half, it felt like we were always facing a long field, which certainly limits what you want to do and can do offensively,” Diaz said.\n\n\r\n              <hl2>Pearman steps up</hl2>\r\n              Swinney said Pearman bounced back from struggles after being pressed into action last week, saying he “prepared like Joe Montana this week.”\n\n\r\n              “I knew he'd be ready,” Swinney said.\n\n\r\n              When this one was over, Pearman had an exuberant fist-pumping celebration in front of the main section of Clemson fans in a corner of Wallace Wade Stadium, then shared congratulatory hugs with multiple teammates.\n\n\r\n              “I”m just happy for Trent, man,\" Lawson said. “He's been sitting dudes for the last five years. ... I'm glad he took advantage of his opportunity and showed everyone that boy can play ball.”\n\n\r\n              <hl2>Record kicks</hl2>\r\n              Clemson’s <a href=\"http://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5119662/nolan-hauser\" target=\"_new\">Nolan Hauser</a> kicked two field goals, including a 39-yarder with 1:01 left. That pushed him to the ACC single-season record with his 31st successful kick.\n\n\r\n              <hl2>Up next</hl2>\r\n              Clemson: The Tigers host in-state rival South Carolina Nov. 28 during the Thanksgiving weekend.\n\n\r\n              Duke: The Blue Devils visit No. 10 Wake Forest Nov. 28 to close the regular season in a long-running instate series.\n\n\r\n              ------\n\n\r\n              <a href=\"https://na01.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fapnews.com%2Fnewsletters&data=05%7C02%7C%7Cdcd4c927a3014947713f08def3da887f%7C84df9e7fe9f640afb435aaaaaaaaaaaa%7C1%7C0%7C639216317087773713%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=592GG7Tx9gHi6vEFt3ytKh9NsJsnsJX5%2FPfFbPmAML8%3D&reserved=0\">Sign up for Top 25 poll alerts here</a>. Visit <a href=\"https://na01.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fapnews.com%2Fhub%2Fap-top-25-college-football-poll&data=05%7C02%7C%7Cdcd4c927a3014947713f08def3da887f%7C84df9e7fe9f640afb435aaaaaaaaaaaa%7C1%7C0%7C639216317087791086%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=ia6nZ9tg%2FZcgm%2BFi8LWTUGjusXBzP7sDOqfdsmjeNig%3D&reserved=0\">the Top 25 here</a> and see AP’s full <a href=\"https://na01.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https%3A%2F%2Fapnews.com%2Fhub%2Fcollege-football&data=05%7C02%7C%7Cdcd4c927a3014947713f08def3da887f%7C84df9e7fe9f640afb435aaaaaaaaaaaa%7C1%7C0%7C639216317087810304%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&sdata=qcVbI2ec6BmMJKNCLT%2FCF5%2FSYMV2T%2F%2F4IEoI